      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_0029bb1_JLcCqp": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_0029bb1",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Understanding Diamond Clarity Grades",
            "text": "<p>When judging clarity, gemmologists consider how visible these characteristics and how much they may affect the diamond’s overall appearance:</p><p><strong>Size:</strong> Larger inclusions or blemishes are generally easier to see and can have a greater effect on the clarity grade.</p><p><strong>Quantity:</strong> The number of clarity characteristics also matters. Several small inclusions may influence the grade just as much as one more noticeable mark.</p><p><strong>Location:</strong> Characteristics positioned beneath the table or close to the centre of the stone are usually more significant than those near the outer edges, as they can be more visible and may interfere more with light performance.</p><p><strong>Type:</strong> Not all inclusions are equal. Crystals, feathers, clouds, cavities, and other natural features can each affect a diamond differently depending on their form and severity.</p><p><strong>Visibility:</strong> The contrast between a clarity characteristic and the surrounding diamond is also important. A darker or more obvious inclusion will usually have a stronger impact than one that blends into the stone.</p><p>Assessing clarity requires trained judgement, as these factors work together rather than in isolation. The Gemological Institute of America (GIA) clarity system provides a consistent framework for grading diamonds, helping buyers compare stones with greater confidence.</p>",
            "heading_size_desktop": 30,
            "text_size_desktop": 18,
            "padding_left_desktop": 380,
            "padding_right_desktop": 380,
            "spacing_desktop": 40,
            "heading_size_mobile": 20,
